\begin{document}

\title{A Cost-Benefit Study of Template Metaprogramming}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{1\textsuperscript{st} Miguel Veganzones Parellada}
    \IEEEauthorblockA{\textit{Technical University of Munich} \\
        % \textit{Technical University of Munich}\\
        Munich, Germany \\
    miguel.veganzones@tum.de}
}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\end{abstract}

\begin{abstract}
Modern high-performance computing applications rely on efficient abstractions
to balance performance and usability.
This work investigates how increasing compiler visibility through
compile-time information affects the generated code and execution efficiency
of numerical kernels.
In particular, we use template metaprogramming and constexpr evaluation to
progressively specialize implementations of the same algorithm by moving
information from runtime to compile time.

The impact of specialization is evaluated by comparing tensor contraction kernel
with various degrees of specialization.
The evaluation considers execution time, compilation overhead, generated code
characteristics, code size, and the extent to which compile-time information
enables compiler optimizations such as constant propagation.
The results illustrate the trade-off between abstraction, generality, and the
ability of the compiler to generate efficient machine code.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
    template metaprogramming, \cpp{}, code generation, compile-time
    specialization, constexpr evaluation
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Modern processors are increasingly complex and utilizing them effectively
requires close collaboration with the compiler.
The code must expose sufficient information to the compiler so that
optimizations and can be applied.
However, high-level abstractions often hide structural properties of a program
until runtime, limiting the transformations available during compilation.

\cpp{} aims to provide a simple and direct mapping to hardware, and zero-cost
abstractions \cite[p.~2]{stroustrup_foundations_2011} as its core philosophy.
One of the main design goals of the zero-overhead principle is that the cost of
using high-level abstractions should be zero, as compared to its hand-crafted
counterpart \cite{noauthor_zero-overhead_nodate}.

Template metaprogramming allows extending this principle to reducing the cost of
abstractions beyond what a programmer could produce by hand.
This is possible by allowing and guiding the compiler into generating extremely
thin layers of abstraction whose complexity is resolved at compile time.

Compiler visibility into the source and knowledge of values and types determines
the transformations that the compiler can perform, and the constructs that it
can be directed into producing.

Compile time values are accessible to the compiler in constexpr contexts, which
can be used in code generation (embdedding literals into assembly immediate
arguments\footnote{For example: \texttt{add eax, 1}. Here the integral literal 1
has been embedded in source, while \texttt{eax} contains a runtime value stored
in a register}).

Similarly, templated types allow the compiler to compute types at compile time
\cite[p.~6]{stroustrup_foundations_2011}.
Template metaprogramming techniques exploit this mechanism to compute arbitrary
types and guide code generation.
Template metaprogramming is used in highly-optimized applications to afford
efficient abstractions \cite{hendricks_lessons_2025}.

Compile time information must be present in the source for the compiler to be
able to use it.
This applies most often to values, but the same thing applies to types.
Compile time information can be lowered to runtime information in a nested
context, reducing the compiler's visibility moving forward within that nested
context.
This process is irreversible, and defines a flow of information from its source
to its destination, where it is eventually consumed.

Compile time information is can be particularly useful in specific
applications such as numerical kernels, where the structure of the computation
often depends on properties such as dimensions, data layout, and iteration
patterns.
Tensor contraction is selected as the reference application in this work because
it exposes several independent dimensions of compile-time specialization. Tensor
rank, tensor dimensions, and contraction indices can either be represented
dynamically or encoded directly into the generated implementation, allowing
controlled comparison between different levels of compiler visibility.

Please find the source code of this project at
\href{https://github.com/MiguelVeganzones/constexpr_propagation}{\texttt{constexpr\_propagation}}.
\footnote{\url{https://github.com/MiguelVeganzones/constexpr_propagation}}

\section{Compile-Time Information}

This section describes how compile time information can affect code generation
to understand the potential benefits that we aim to quantify in this work.

Comnpute a type at compile time


\subsection{Compile Time Work}

It is often desirable to perform computation at compile time.
Often we aim to compute values such that they do not need to be recomputed at
runtime; computing types is also often desirable for type safety and efficient
type specialization \cite[p.~6]{stroustrup_foundations_2011}.

\subsubsection{Move computation from runtime to compile time}

Moving any computation from runtime to compile time should increase compile
times in favor of runtime.
The compiler executes \cpp{} in an internal interpreter, so it is significantly
more limited than the runtime  \cite{noauthor_constant_nodate}.

\subsubsection{Avoid redundant computation at runtime}

Often, efficient implementations expect work to be done by the caller, such as
an output parameter in matrix multiplication.
This often happens because work performed behind an API call must be done per
call, not per caller.
Moving this computation to the compile time can enable efficient and clean APIs.

\subsubsection{Compile-Time Complexity}

Cost of recompilation increases as the compiler is responsible for more work.
Template metaprogramming techniques can become a burden if recompilation is
frequent.
This often happens with heavily templated Boost libraries.

Modern \cpp{} standards have improved on this aspect by providing explicit
language support, with constructs such as \texttt{if constexpr} and
\texttt{template for}.

\subsection{Code Generation}

Code generation is influenced significantly by compiler visibility and the
flexibility required for the code.

\subsubsection{Enable optimizations}
Hard-coding information about the size of a loop can enable optimizations such
as automatic loop unrolling and auto-vectorization.

\subsubsection{Code Size}
Code size if affected in various ways by template metaprogramming, most often
code grows due to template duplication.
However, templated code can often be simpler and smaller once compiled.

\subsubsection{Type Simplification}
The use of templates and compile time values can allow using types with stronger
invariants, such as replacing a \texttt{std::vector} with a \texttt{std::array}.
Properties that are often desirable are \texttt{triviality}, \texttt{standard
layout}, \texttt{trivial copyability}.

\subsubsection{Compile time contracts}

Compile time computation can be also used to enforce contracts at compile time,
which is a strong invariant that the runtime cannot provide.

\subsection{Architectural}

\subsubsection{Reduce memory operations}

Assembly immediate arguments bypass the normal load path, and are rather fetched
by the fronted along with their instruction.
This reduces the overall number of load operations in the assembly.
What type categories and bit widths are allowed is architecture-dependent.
Note that Intel 64 (Intel's version of x86-64) supports small integral and no
floating point immediates, while AMD64 does also support floating point
immediates to some extent.
% Immediate arguments are inevitably fetched with their instruction, bypassing
% loads from the data pipeline to be fetched.

\subsubsection{Reduce function arguments}

Compile time information or uncertainty determines what regions of the memory
model can be used.
The heap is the least restrictive and can be used at runtime, but with higher
costs of allocation.
Stack memory is fast, but the compiler must know the size of objects, and their
lifetime is context-determined.
The most restrictive section is are the static/globals, which have fixed
locations in the binary.
These can be constants or read/write values.
The static/global constants (located in \texttt{.rdata}) are the most powerful
for the compiler.

% Static global data region has predictable locations
% Heap is clearly in a disadvantage
% Stack arguments must be input arguments as their location is unknown to an
% arbitrary caller.
% Static/global symbols in \texttt{.rdata} or \texttt{.data} have predictable
% locations, which can potentially be hardcoded in the load instruction.

\subsubsection{Reduce register pressure}
Immediate arguments reduce register pressure as they never become part of the
in-flight or architectural state, but are rather consumed and discarded.

\subsubsection{Read-only data and coherence protocols}
Read only data regions (\texttt{.rdata}) are simpler to manage by a multithreaded architecture as
it promises stronger invariants.
Race conditions and initialization failures are not an issue for compile time
values.

\section{Design Space}

This project aims to evaluate the tradeoffs of heavy template metaprogramming
and constexpr evaluation.
The design choice we focus on is: runtime versus compile-time parameters.
This decision will be evaluated on its impact on flexibility against
performance.

\section{Compute Kernel}

The extent to which compile-time information can be exploited effectively is
highly application-dependent.
Therefore, a reference application must be chosen where the availability of such
information has a direct impact on both the algorithmic structure and
implementation strategy.

Tensor contraction has been selected as the reference compute kernel due to its
relevance in scientific computing workloads, as well as its ability to exploit
compile time information.
A tensor contraction generalizes matrix multiplication by combining two tensors
over one or more shared dimensions while preserving the remaining dimensions as
output dimensions.
The operation can be expressed as a summation over a set of contraction indices:

\[
C_{i,j,\dots} = \sum_{k,l,\dots} A_{i,k,\dots}B_{k,j,\dots}
\]

Unlike fixed-rank linear algebra operations, tensor contractions require
handling additional structural information, including tensor rank, dimension
sizes, memory layout, and contraction indices.
When this information is only available at runtime, the implementation must rely
on dynamic mechanisms such as runtime shape traversal, and generalized iteration
schemes with a multi-index.
In contrast, when this information is available at compile time, the
implementation can specialize the generated kernel by resolving iteration
structures and intermediate values during compilation.

This makes tensor contraction a suitable benchmark for evaluating the impact
of compile-time information.
For this, we consider three variants that are explained below.

\section{Implementation}

We consider three levels of compile time information that the implementation can
exploit in a meaningful way.
Each level will build on top of the previous one, always elevating information
from runtime to compile time.
These levels are denominated \textit{runtime rank}, \textit{compile-time size}
and \textit{compile-time sizes} (\level{v1}, \level{v2}, \level{v3}
respectively), according to how the available information affects each
implementation.

These can be though of as different specification standards with different
requirements for compliance.

\subsection{Runtime Rank (\level{v1})}

This level specifies the lowest degree of compile-time information expected by
each of the implementations.
It requires static typing and static binding.
This is a sensible baseline as it is often taken for granted.

Static typing implies that the concrete types being used are known to the
compiler (\ie{} no \texttt{std::any} or \texttt{void*}).
Static binding implies that function calls are resolved at compile time (\ie{}
no virtual dispatch or function pointers).

In this context, the compiler has full visibility of the concrete
types and call structure, enabling optimization across function boundaries and
efficient code generation.
However, no algorithm-specific information such as ranks, dimensions or
contraction patterns will be accessible during code generation.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v1::tensor} (\level{t1})}

This variant must use the heap to store both its data and its metadata
The data buffer will be a contiguous buffer of \texttt{T}.
Similarly, the metadata buffer will contain the tensor sizes and strides for
efficient access with a multi-index.
Accessing these buffers through a pointer indirection does not meaningfully
affect runtime on its own, as compared to the heap.
However, we will have 7 different allocations\footnote{Two allocations for each
    tensor and one for the contraction index set. Each tensor uses two buffers
    in the heap, these could be merged into one, but the additional complexity
was deemed unimportant.} which will demand more memory subsystem resources.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v1::contraction} (\level{c1})}

Since the rank of the tensors is unknown at compile time, the first-level
contraction implementation uses a multi-index to iterate over the tensors.
The multi-index uses an internal \texttt{for} loop for each increment,
introducing significant overhead in iteration.

\subsection{Compile-Time Rank (v2)}

The second level requires that the rank of the tensors and the contraction
degree be known at compile time.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v2::tensor} (\level{t2})}

Knowing the rank of the tensor allows the strides and sizes of the tensors
involved to be stored within the tensor object itself.
Thanks to this compile time information, the metadata of the tensor will be
stored in the stack, rather than the heap, and accessible without a pointer
indirection.\footnote{Moving data from the stack to the heap also removes a
costly memory allocation, but these are not included in the benchmark loop.}
The data itself must still be heap-allocated, however, as the size of each
dimension is not known.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v2::contraction} (\level{c2})}

Knowing the rank of the tensors significantly simplifies iterating over the
tensors\footnote{The rank of a tensor corresponds to the number of nested for
loops required to iterate over it in a structured way.}.
This implementation sets up two sets of nested \texttt{for} loops using template
metaprogramming, removing the need to iterate using a multi-index.

The internal structure of this iteration scheme is shown in \Cref{lst:iteration}.
Given a range of sizes of size \texttt{Rank}, this function stamps one nested
loop for each dimension and keeps track of the indices per dimension.
In the inner most loop, a callback is invoked, adding the indices as an
additional argument.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Nested loop iteration}, label={lst:iteration}]
template <
    std::integral Size_Type,
    Size_Type I,
    Size_Type Rank>
constexpr auto shaped_for_impl(
    std::ranges::sized_range auto const& sizes,
    std::ranges::sized_range auto&       idxs,
    auto&&                               fn,
    auto&&...                            args
) noexcept -> void
{
    if constexpr (I == Rank)
    {
        std::invoke(
            std::forward<decltype(fn)>(fn),
            std::forward<decltype(args)>(args)...,
            idxs
        );
        return;
    }

    for (idxs[I] = 0; idxs[I] != sizes[I];
         ++idxs[I])
    {
        shaped_for_impl<Size_Type, I + 1, Rank>(
            sizes,
            idxs,
            std::forward<decltype(fn)>(fn),
            std::forward<decltype(args)>(args)...
        );
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Compile-Time Sizes (v3)}

The third level requires that the sizes of the tensors and the indices to
contract over, be known at compile time.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v3::tensor} (\level{t3})}

This affects the tensor implementation in two major ways.
For once, the data in the tensor may be stored in the stack, significantly
improving locality, specially for small tensors.
Additionally, tensor sizes and strides become part of the type, reducing the
size of each instance.

A limit of 128 KB per tensor has been used to conditionally store the data in
the heap to prevent stack overflow.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v3::contraction} (\level{c3})}

Knowing the size of the tensors allows propagating these values into the loop
limits into the two sets of nested \texttt{for} loops.
This enables inlining the loop limits into the assembly as integral immediate
arguments, and enables aggressive loop unrolling and vectorization.

Additionally, iteration functions now require less input arguments, since the
location of static data is known at compile time.
This change makes feasible computing the target linear index into each tensor
during iteration, removing an inner product computation between the strides and
the multi-index on each tensor access.\footnote{This same optimization was
    unsuccessful for the \texttt{v2::contraction} iteration scheme, presumably for
the significant increase in function arguments.}

\subsection{Signatures}

\Cref{lst:signatures} shows the signatures of the tensor types and the
tensor contraction.\footnote{Names have been simplified.}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Signatures}, label={lst:signatures}]
using T = double;       // value_type
std::size_t N = \* *\;  // Rank
std::size_t Sx = \* *\; // N Sizes

// Tensor signatures
using t1 = v1::tensor<T>};
using t2 = v2::tensor<T, N>};
using t3 = v3::tensor<T, S1, ..., SN>;

// Tensor contraction singatures
v1::contract(v1::compatible auto const& A, 
             v1::compatible auto const& B, 
             v1::compatible auto &      C,
             v1::index_set  auto const& cis);

v2::contract(v2::compatible auto const& A, 
             v2::compatible auto const& B, 
             v2::compatible auto &      C,
             v2::index_set  auto const& cis);

v3::contract<CIS>(v3::compatible auto const& A, 
                  v3::compatible auto const& B, 
                  v3::compatible auto &      C);
\end{lstlisting}

To allow generic use of the contraction implementations, template arguments are
deduced from the input arguments.
Only \texttt{v3::contraction} requires an additional \ac{nttp} argument as it is
necessary to determine the shape of the output tensor, which must be known at
compile time.

\subsection{Compatibility}

Contraction and tensor implementations of different levels are compatible
because each level extends the previous one with stricter requirements and
compile time information can be lowered to runtime information.
Since the opposite is not true, compatibility goes in one direction, making
\texttt{v3::tensor} compatible with any contraction implementation, 
\texttt{v2::tensor} compatible with \texttt{v2::contraction} and
\texttt{v1::contraction}, and \texttt{v1::tensor} only compatible with
\texttt{v1::contraction}.

This will be used to assess what part of the performance improvements can be
attributed to changes in the tensor implementation or the contraction
implementation.

% t3 meets the requirements, but not the limitations of v1, and can, therefore, be used by c1.

\section{Experimental Setup}

All benchmarks were conducted on a system with the following specifications
shown in \Cref{tab:system}.

\begin{table}[htbp]
    \caption{Hardware and software environment used for benchmarking.}
    \begin{center}
        \begin{tabular}{ll}
            \hline
            Operating System & Arch Linux \\
            Kernel & 6.18.35-1-lts \\
            Compiler & GCC 16.1.1 \\
            Language Standard & C++26 \\
            CPU & Intel Core i7-9750H \\
            DRAM & 16 GB DDR4-2667 MT/s \\
            Cores / Threads & 6 / 12 \\
            Base Frequency & 2.6 GHz \\
            \hline
        \end{tabular}
        \label{tab:system}
    \end{center}
\end{table}

The compiler was configured with aggressive optimization settings to focus
benchmark differences on code generation:
\begin{verbatim}
-O3 -fno-exceptions -march=native -mavx
-fno-math-errno -ffast-math
-fno-trapping-math -flto
\end{verbatim}
These optimization flags enable high-level, whole-program compiler
optimizations, target the native CPU architecture, enable AVX vector
instructions, allow more aggressive floating-point transformations.

\section{Results}

Elevating information to the compile time leads to a series of tradeoffs, and
must be evaluated with metrics that capture these.
We will primarily use the following set of objective

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{runtime}: Main metric to measure code efficiency
    \item \texttt{compilation time}: Measure how compilation times increase with
        compile time computation and template metaprogramming.
    \item \texttt{code size}: Measure the impact of template metaprogramming in
        binary size.
\end{itemize}

Additionally, we will look at the generated assembly to observe the effects of
\texttt{constant propagation} and the presence of immediate arguments.

\subsection{Runtime}

Most often, compile time information is exploited to improve performance in
expenses of flexibility.
Runtime is, therefore, the most important metric we will analyze in order to
measure potential gains.
Wether those gains are justifiable depends on the application and should be
carefully considered.

The benchmark consists of 63 contractions of tensors of various sizes and ranks.
Sizes range from 2 to 64 per dimension, ranks range from 2 to 6, and contracted
indices range from 1 to 4 pairs.
Not all combinations are present in the benchmark as exponential growth rapidly
exhausts system resources.

Runtime is plotted against the number of floating point operations needed for
the naive implementation we are using, which amounts to about two floating point
operations per inner-most loop iteration.

For the following plots, C1, C2, C3 stand for the three contraction
implementations, and T1, T2, T3 stand for the three tensor implementations.

Runtime has been measured using Google Benchmark, and mean time over 10
repeated runs is reported.\footnote{Note that Google Benchmark performs iterations
until convergence in each run.}

\subsubsection{\texttt{v1::contraction}}

\Cref{fig:c1-runtime} shows how the tensor implementation impacts performance
for the same contraction algorithm that does not exploit the specific tensor
implementation.
The difference lies in the overhead of accessing tensor data and metadata.

The improvement obtained by replacing \texttt{v1::tensor} with \texttt{v2::tensor} is
relatively small.
The effects responsible for the improvement should be improved locality for the
metadata, which is now stored in the stack along the metadata of the other tensors.

Using \texttt{v3::tensor} as the tensor implementation leads to more significant
improvements.
Now, tensor indexing should be much more efficient, as the strides are compile
time constants that the compiler can inline.
Additionally, tensor data is stored in the stack for small tensors, which
improves performance for small enough tensors significantly.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/C1.pdf}}
    \caption{\texttt{v1::contraction} performance scaling with each compatible tensor.}
    \label{fig:c1-runtime}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{\texttt{v2::contraction}}

This implementation can use the rank of the tensors and contraction index set at
compile time, bypassing the need to iterate with multi-indices and removing
allocations entirely from the benchmark loop.
Here, \texttt{v3::tensor} improves upon the baseline with better locality for
small sizes and more efficient data access.
\Cref{fig:c2-runtime} shows the results graphically.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/C2.pdf}}
    \caption{\texttt{v2::contraction} performance scaling with each compatible tensor.}
    \label{fig:c2-runtime}
\end{figure}

For sizes below a few hundred operations, the benchmarking framework is not able
to accurately measure runtime and we get essentially constant values.
This could be solved by benchmarking natively with a simple stopwatch; however,
these examples are pathological and unimportant.

\subsubsection{\texttt{v3::contraction}}

This contraction implementation is only compatible with \texttt{v3::tensor}, as
it expects compile time sizes from the input and output tensors.
For completeness, \Cref{fig:c3-runtime} shows the scaling of this implementation
graphically.
This implementation propagates constants to the loop control limits 

