\subsection{Compile Time}

Template metaprogramming demands the compiler to perform significant type
manipulations, and constexpr evaluation requires the compiler to execute
unoptimized \cpp{} code in an internal interpreter.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centerline{\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/build_times.pdf}}
    \caption{Example of a figure caption.}
    \label{fig:build-time}
\end{figure}

\Cref{fig:build-time} shows that \texttt{v3::tensor} introduces a significant
compile time time overhead, increasing compile times by up to three times in the
current examples.

This increase in compile time was expected and not particularly problematic for
these small micro benchmarks, but could become a problem in larger, more
sophisticated applications.
The real limitation, however, is that compile time computation cannot scale to
the same order of magnitude as the runtime.
For this reason, compile time computation cannot ever replace runtime
computation, it is rather a complement to it, both need to be balanced within
the context of an application.

\subsection{Code Generation}

In terms of code generation, we will analyze constant propagation into immediate
arguments in the \texttt{v3} variant and code size.

\subsubsection{Assembly Immediates}
We only consider this variant because it is the only implementation where the
compiler has enough information such that it should not embed it everywhere.
This latter point will be illustrated below.

For \texttt{v2}, all of the compile time information provided is explicitly
exploited, and is, therefore, not up to the implementation to embed in the
assembly.
An example of this scenario are the nested \texttt{for} loops shown in
\Cref{lst:iteration}; the implementation is not allowed to choose an
alternative, as we have explicitly stamped those loops.
Similarly, the stack arrays that contain tensor metadata are explicitly
instantiated, so the compiler is forced to use the compile time rank specified
by the \texttt{v2} variant.
The compiler is free to optimize the code in multiple ways that would
essentially remove these constructs we have explicitly requested, but in such
cases the compiler is choosing to transform code that already has these values
embedded.

In \texttt{v3}, however, we, for example, explicitly propagate
\texttt{constexpr} values into the limits of \texttt{for} loops.
If these loop limits where embedded in the assembly, there would need to be an
assembly block for each such loop, as each must contain their own loop limits.
However, since we are using regular, runtime loops\footnote{As opposed to the compile
time recursion loop used in \Cref{lst:iteration} to stamp \texttt{for} loops.},
we are not forcing the compiler into stamping many variations of the same loop,
rather, we are providing the resources required to do it.
The compiler will then decide whether to inline constexpr values (such as the
loop limits) in the assembly based on its tradeoffs, primarily runtime
performance and code size.

In the generated assembly of \texttt{v3} examples, we have observed that loop
limits have often been embedded in the assembly as immediate arguments, while
tensor strides have not been embedded in similar logic used for linear index
calculation.
This seems to be because the compiler is actively exploiting a regularity
present in most of the benchmark data points.
In particular, we mostly used tensors with the same size per dimension, which
significantly reduces the times that code duplication would happen by embedding
the value (if the same value is always embedded, then no duplication is
necessary).

Even these regular tensors have different strides per dimension, which explains
why the compiler decided to load the strides from memory at runtime to reuse the
same code, instead of stamping variants for every stride size.

\subsubsection{Code Size}
No significant source code size differences have been observed across
implementations.

